=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r7
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on R7, not R6. The team's hypothesis is misdirected: R6's OSPF
process is fully healthy — it holds two Full adjacencies (with R4 on eth0 and
R5 on eth1) and its eth2 interface (67.1.1.1/8, segment Y toward R7) is up and
correctly configured in area 1. R6's "neighbor down" log is a symptom, not a
cause: it is simply recording that R7 never came up as a peer.

The root cause is that R7's ospfd daemon has crashed and is in a FAILED state
(`service frr status` on R7 returns "Status of ospfd: FAILED ... failed!";
watchfrr is also failed). No ospfd process appears in R7's process table. R7's
/etc/frr/frr.conf is intact and correct — it declares router-id 67.1.1.2 and
advertises 67.0.0.0/8, 70.0.0.0/8, and the four 17.1.x.0/24 loopback prefixes
into area 1. However, because the daemon is not running, none of these networks
are injected into OSPF, R7 never sends Hellos on eth0 (67.1.1.2/8), and the
R6–R7 adjacency never forms. As a consequence, R1's OSPF table is missing all
R7-originated routes: the 17.1.x.0/24 loopbacks and 70.0.0.0/8 (R7's LAN on
eth1) are completely absent.

Corrective fix: on R7, run `service frr restart` (or `service frr start` if
watchfrr remains stuck) to bring ospfd back up. Once ospfd starts and loads
/etc/frr/frr.conf, it will form a Full adjacency with R6 via eth0 and
re-advertise the missing prefixes throughout area 1, restoring the routes on R1.
=== END ===
